+# Per-candidate geological write-up — the shipped file (Phase 2)
+
+**Provenance.** This document was rendered on 2026-09-26 by the review
+workspace's `tools/phase2_narrative.py` from the measured dossier
+`current_shipped_geology_2026-09-26.json` (official bytes hash-pinned, every
+raster gated; no official byte changed between the two measurements) and copied
+into this entry as the per-candidate geological record (NEXT_STEPS item 9).
+
+**Grouping rule differs from `CANDIDATES.md`.** This write-up covers the **180
+groups of >= 200 closed pixels** (5x5 closing) — the large objects a geological
+read can attach measured indicators to. `CANDIDATES.md` inventories the finer
+**1,380 components of >= 8 px** (14,626 km of candidate line) from the same
+shipped bytes, with full per-component geometry in
+`data/evidence/candidates.json`. The remainder of the shipped emission (26,040
+emitted pixels in 2,409 small groups, plus 93 pixels not retained by the
+closing) is inventoried there, not called faults, and not interpreted here —
+real narrow faults could still lie in that remainder. Both documents describe
+the same file (sha256 `33cec71ff0…`); read them together.
+
+---
+
+# Phase-2 candidate narratives — shipped file — 2026-09-26
+
+Per-candidate geological reasoning for every >=200-closed-px group the model
+flags in the **shipped** submission (SHA-256 `33cec71ff00b3f32d0d59c81c156f3f1488ffef46baa4b6499094e24ea1875ab`, first 12: `33cec71ff00b`),
+rendered by `tools/phase2_narrative.py` from the measured dossier
+`current_shipped_geology_2026-09-26.json`. The mask is a hypothesis list, not
+a fault map.
+
+**Regional frame (cited sources only).** The GeoDAWN survey area covers
+ground "associated with the Walker Lane and western Great Basin"
+([competition About page](https://www.drivendata.org/competitions/306/competition-doe-gems/page/968/)).
+The Walker Lane is documented as a belt of northwest-trending, right-lateral
+shear ([USGS DDS-58 ch. I](https://pubs.usgs.gov/dds/dds-058/Ch_I.pdf));
+Basin-and-Range extension in Nevada is expressed as northerly-trending
+high-angle normal faults forming north-trending basins and ranges
+([USGS OFR 67-11](https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/ofr6711);
+[USGS FAQ on normal faults](https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-a-fault-and-what-are-different-types)).
+These sources justify using NW ~300-345 deg, N ~345-25 deg and NE ~25-70 deg
+as *interpretation aids* for measured azimuths. They do not identify any
+specific structure.
+
+**What every entry below is:** a measured geometry + measured signal-decile
+support + measured distance class + the depth status, composed into a
+hypothesis with its counterinterpretation. **What none of them is:** a
+verified fault, a depth estimate, or a prediction of hidden score. Tilt-depth
+is not estimable on the supplied bands (dead magnetic tilt); where this file
+says a group is 'isolated', isolation is a distance heuristic, not a
+discovery. Under the staff masking rule, mass within 300 m of the training
+labels is not exempt from false-positive cost.
+
+**Measured headline for this file:** 180 candidates; agreement histogram {0: 66, 1: 81, 2: 27, 3: 6} (families of 6) — only 6 of 180 reach 3/6 families, and **no candidate reaches the 'provisional' tier** (needs >=3 families AND elongation >= 2 AND >= 1,000 emitted px). The shipped flags are, at group level, weakly supported; treat the whole file as a broad anomaly surface, not a line list.
+
